once but  the process of restoration needs to be  one that is done and tactically  strategically with due attention to a  popular opinion and that's exactly I  think what Lincoln was doing right  lincoln did I think ultimately and in  some sense Lincoln was an abolitionist  his view was that ultimately we are  going to do away with slavery but his  process before the war especially his  his approach was you contain it you keep  it out of the territories then you  convinced the southern states especially  that they need to begin to take up a  policy of gradual and compensated  emancipation and Lincoln propose to do  that through existing political  institutions especially through the the  state legislatures and indeed Lincoln  was trying to get Congress to promote  this among the states well into the  Civil War so I think again a Lincoln's  policy of prudence would be the one to  want to follow  from Jon &amp; Hermitage Tennessee Lincoln's  second inaugural and many other of his  speeches are full of references to the  divine what was Lincoln's understanding  of the public role of religion um again  I think Lakeland although the American  founders established through Z a regime  in which we had a civil and political  and religious liberty and they do have  toleration and pluralism was certainly  the the predominant one at the time of  the American Founding nevertheless this  certainly did not exclude the the place  or presence of religion in public life  right the separation of church and state  and the idea of no established churches  is one thing but the idea that people  are not able to bring their religious  views into public life and to the  formation of public policy was far from  what the founders had in view and from  what what what Lincoln had into you the  question of Lincoln's own religious  commitments just sort of putting that to  the side Lincoln was aware that the  United States was a profoundly Christian  country Lincoln for example said that he  supported an effort to amend the  Constitution to explicitly state that  the United States was a Christian  country so Lincoln was perfectly aware  of the the good that religion could play  in public life and the second inaugural  of course is the the classic example of  that most concise explanation to this  day of what the Civil War was was  ultimately about and Lincoln seeing it  as God's judgment on the country for the  the sin of slavery again toward the end  of his life Lincoln had been moved I  think to see more more profoundly the  ultimately religious dimension of what  was going on in America  Phil from Scottsdale rights you talked a  lot about Lincoln's ideas what was  Lincoln like as an as a man as an  individual what made him great well it's  like I think like most great men Lincoln  was very hard to actually get to know  and he had few intimate friends although  he was again a very gregarious and very  public person a great storyteller a  great serac on tour Stephen Douglas was  you know sort of afraid of being you  need to debate with Lincoln because of  his just a wonderful effect upon people  yet he remains something of an enigmatic  figure as a man to this day as perhaps  all all great men are um what made him  great from again did the perspective of  the American political and  constitutional experience was his again  it's I think his willingness to resist  the temptation of power and to remain  committed to what he put it in one of  his early speeches as the preservation  of our political institutions Lincoln  himself was aware of the great danger  throughout human history of people who  desired greatness and Lincoln was  certainly a man who desired greatness  and ambition his law partner William  Herndon said that his ambition was a  little engine that knew no rest but the  Lincoln was not going to destroy the  Republic for the sake of establishing  his own greatness that that the problem  that Republican governments have  especially the American regime is that  the founders established something that  was you know was good and what we needed  to do was to preserve it and perpetuate  it Lincoln said but there are some  people those of the race of the lion and  the tribe of the eagle or something like  that wouldn't be enough for them to do  that just to pass on the blessings of  liberty from your ancestors to your  descendants is not going to be enough to  satisfy the desire of really ambitious  men people like like Napoleon some  people Lincoln said in order to  establish their own greatness would  would flinch it nothing even if it meant  the emancipation of slaves or the  enslavement of  reman Eddie and Lincoln's detractors  have said that in the speech he was sort  of prophesying his own destruction of  the of the traditional constitutional  system and the establishment of a new  one but I think Lincoln's greatness lies  in the fact that he did not do that and  that despite the again cataclysmic Civil  War the United States came through that  with a returned a clearer statement of  what the founding principles were and  that Lincoln was able to again pass on a  constitutional republic to the next  generation of Americans I think if you  look at the Civil War the effect that it  had upon american society in the  american economy and all that work we're  pretty great but nevertheless after the  Civil War what's remarkable is how  quickly politics and constitutional  government returned to its antebellum a  way of way of doing things that you  didn't establish as state of them that  Bismarck was doing in Russia or Cavour  and Italy the United States had a  million-man army at the end of the Civil  War but we didn't keep it after the war  was over everyone went home the great  expansion of executive power during the  Civil War and Lincoln as I made the  argument was properly vigorously  exercising his powers as commander in  chief but nevertheless you return to the  sort of normal condition of legislative  predominance and executive sort of  deference in the years after the Civil  War so Lincoln did not think in the  Republicans generally did not take the  Civil War as an opportunity to  fundamentally transform the American  Republic and to again in peacetime  continue not the extraordinary powers of  wartime 20th century liberalism is just  the opposite you hear the progressives  and liberals in the 20th century  constantly trying to talk about domestic  social policy in Marshall terms right  Theodore Roosevelt does this all the  time franklin delano roosevelt and his  first inaugural talking about the  depression uses explicitly military  analogy and you hear this all the time  today people talking about the war on  drugs or the war on poverty of the war  on on whatever the grade one of the  great virtues of Lincoln of the  Republicans was they resisted the  opportunities for sort of unlimited  government  that the Civil War presented thank you  very much
 

